{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 16, :comments ["<b>And if with an iron tool he struck him.</b> Why say 'if'? Since if he struck him with stone or wood he is liable, with iron all the more so! Rather, <b>'and if with an iron tool he struck him'</b> was stated to teach that he is liable even with a needle — to teach you that one does not punish from logical inference. Here Rashi explained that iron kills with any amount, even a needle; therefore the Torah did not set a measure for it, writing only 'אשר ימות בו'. And the same applies to molten iron (מברז ששפכו בו) — of this sort it is fit to kill even with a needle, but if he struck him with it, a measure is required. Thus it is taught in a baraita, and thus Rashi explained in the chapter 'אלו הן הנשרפים.'"], :book :numbers, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 35, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:20:45.284748669Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}